Summary and Reflection
The unit testing that was us in this software was aligned by reading, researching, and understanding each of the system requirements that were given. I analyzed each requirement to extract the constriants that were needed. Those constraints were then digitally transferred into coding constructs when wrote out into the validation logic that checks for insuffient data being passed by a parameter to the object. By looking at the system requirement for not allowing more than the maxium or less than the minimuium amount of characters, not containing a null value, and whether or not it was to be updatable; I was able to validate each parameter against the constraint before it could be passed into the object. This step was repeated for all of the objects and service objects that was required to build this system. 
This can be seeen in Task. java on lines 15, 20, and 25; as well as in Appointment. java on lines 16, 21, and 26. The same validation checks were implemented in the add and update methods for each service object. This can be reviewed in the TaskServiceTest. java in the addTask method on lines 19 and 24, and you will notice that the TaskId is not checked because it keeps an internal Id tracker that has its own logic in place to prevent multiple instances of the same TaskId value from being used.
JUnit tests were then written using assertions to provide test coverage to show evidence that the objects and their respective parameters were scoped correctly. This was implemented by instintating an object with accuratly defined values. The next stage was applying assertionsEqual arguments that returned that object’s current values and then compared then against the same values it was instintated with. This can be seen inside the object test for Task.java file on lines 20  - 24, for Contact. java on lines 20 – 26, and for Appointment.java on lines 21 – 26. When the values matched, the object was successfully created and the test was passed. The fail scenario was triggered when anything other then the value that it was instintated with was returned.
Now that the object class was registered as correctly implemented, the next phase was to test the object’s validation logic with assertThrow arguments that passed invalid parameters into the object it was testing. This tested the validation and exception handling by attempting to pass invalid data into the object to verify that an IllegalArgumentException was thrown. The test scenario was successful and considered to pass the test if the correct exception was returned. This testing logic can be followed in ContactTest.javafrom line 31 to 161 as seen in the assertThrows methods sending out a bad parameter that corresponds to each individual test that provides complete 100% testing coverage for that object’s parameter. In Contact.java on lines 18 – 36, this can be reviewed by looking specifically at the if statements and the statement under each. When a value that in not within range of the constriant is passed into the Contact object, the if statements catch the passed in valid and returning the corresponding exception message matching the bad parameter.
I repeated the same constraint testing proceedure for each parameter that had a similiar constriants and organized them by how the constraint was being tested. This was for when reviewing a test that reported anything less than 100% for the testing coverage. When looking into the test coverage, the main test is expanded and seperated into individual results for each of the constraints that were tested. The constraints that passed will show 100% and the failed test showing 0%. When the coverage showed a failed TaskIdTooLong test, it was reporting back that the test that checked if the value of the task object’s ID was to long showed had not returned the expected IllegalAgrumentExectption. This could have be from either   incorrectly constructed validation check or a incorrectly written test in TaskTest.java; both should be checked to enforce the princple of due diligence. 
The test that failed could be found in the TooLong section of test methods for TaskId in TaskTest. This made it much more effictive to find a constraint that had failed, as well as making it more efficent to verify that all paremeter’s scopes where correctly addressed when constructing the tests initially. This was used for all of the object’s test and it can be reviewed in AppointmentTest.java on lines 18, 30, 41, 62, 92, and 113.
I started testing my application before I used JUnit testing the Task.class file. When creating the packages to seperate the tests and objects, I had mixed up my syntax in the structure and caused my object test in TaskTest.java to fail even though it was constructed correctly. After I checked my JUnit coverage to discover it was tested as 0% and then I thought to check my terminal to see if the program had executed without errors. That is how I discovered and squashed my first bug in this application that helped me prove to myself that my efficency and effectiveness at debugging were getting better. 
	I performed many manual syntax checks to remedy the many failed JUnit tests with 0% test coverage. To statically test this application, he “@Test” annotation was applied to the top of each method that was used to test the parameters that were defined before executing the code. This was effiecent in helping me verify that I tested each parameter for the required constraint. I could see extra value in this process when creating a similiar backend system that cannot be manually testined because it will not use interactive interfaces. This also saves money as there is no need to create a demo front-end application only to test your back-end system.
This application was not scoped to implament dynamic testing practices using JUnit. While not used in this application, the special annotation “@TestFactory” is used to dynamically and systematically test with various parameters at different stages of execution. The process of building an application is streamlined by being able to utilize the current libraries availble for testing.
Beyond this application of testing, we have vulnerability testing. That processes is automated by scanning the componets and their dependancies being used to be assured as being secured. This process holds great value because if you can prevent a product from being broken, you can prevent the loss of any potentential gains.
This entireity of this project forced me into the role of a tester. I fostered this and built on it with my own knowledge and dislipline as to apply security and stability into my testing. By applying testing practices for validation exploits and parameter manipulation, I was able to align what I had learned with what I knew. This methodology has helped me form new concepts of thought when it comes to building software.
I had ran into the issue of not being able to test a functionality of a application. This was previously resolved by constructing a makeshift system or implementing a user interface to test the functionality. That prevented me from applying that time toward the needed functionality. This can reduce effectiviness and effiency in a production environment, furthering adding to the project’s technical debt.
The bias of testing one’s own code could be very problematic. An example that comes to mind is one of a developer who tests their own code. They may not be able to think outside of the box when it comes to testing. They created it with a thought, and who is to say that their thought contained every attribute that could be used. What if they think that their program cannot be broken and will only be used in one way? That in itself is ignorance because even as we know that we know not what it is that we do not know; we still think we know it all. This is why we have a specialized tester role. That represents the value of another person’s perspective when testing is needed.
I limit my bias when writing code with my discipline to my practice and by thinking that the code is not my code. Code should be tested, broken, remedied, and reviewed to reduce any vulnerabilities or flaws within. I wear the mask of many roles while creating code, and then I wear them again when testing and reviewing that code. I prioritize the functinality of the requirements needed over the value of my person preferences. Before when I wrote code, I manually inspect it for syntax and validation errors. Now when I write code, in addition to my normal process I will be thinking about how I can break that code. 
The importance of being disciplined and holding true to that dicipline is unparrelled in this industry. As a software engineer, there is nothing that will ruin your reputation faster than producing unformatted, non-functional, or sloopy code. This can be prevented by aligning oneself with some of the various principles we have learned in this course. By testing your code before it in production, you can reduce reputatuional damage as well as technical debt in your projects. 
As a developer working on a project owned by another, I certainly do not want to create an issue that leads to more technical debt than I am worth. However, I work pro-bono for a non-profit, so any technical debt that is occured isn’t only reflected on me it hurts the orginaization, our cause, and the people who need our service. This is why I do not cut corners, it is not merely because my reputation as a secure software engineer could be in question. It is the sanctity and security of the mission and of the people who I serve that drive my commitment to my disclipline. That should hold true for us all, for as we read in this week discussion; even the actions of developer have consequences.
